𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟱/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽𝘀 & 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗼𝗹 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

Continuing my #30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I focused on Loops and Control Flow — concepts that make Ansible playbooks more dynamic and reduce repetitive tasks.

🔄 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽𝘀

Instead of writing the same task multiple times, we can use loop.

- name: Install packages
  ansible.builtin.apt:
    name: "{{ item }}"
    state: present
  loop:
    - nginx
    - git
    - curl

One task → multiple items. ⚡

📋 𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗢𝘃𝗲𝗿 𝗗𝗶𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗲𝘀

Loops become even more useful when working with structured data:

users:
  - name: devuser
    state: present
  - name: testuser
    state: present

Then:

- name: Manage users
  ansible.builtin.user:
    name: "{{ item.name }}"
    state: "{{ item.state }}"
  loop: "{{ users }}"

🎯 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗼𝗹 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗶𝗻 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲

We can control how tasks execute using:

✔️ when → Conditional execution
✔️ loop → Repeat tasks
✔️ until → Retry until a condition is met
✔️ retries → Number of retry attempts
✔️ delay → Time between retries
✔️ failed_when → Define custom failure conditions
✔️ changed_when → Control whether a task reports changed

🔁 𝗥𝗲𝘁𝗿𝘆 𝗘𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲

For situations where an application or service may take some time to become ready:

- name: Check application
  ansible.builtin.uri:
    url: http://localhost:8080
    status_code: 200
  register: result
  until: result.status == 200
  retries: 5
  delay: 10

Ansible will retry the task instead of failing immediately. 🔥

💡 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗧𝗵𝗶𝘀 𝗠𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘀

Good automation isn't just about executing tasks.

It's about controlling when, how many times, and under what conditions those tasks should run.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Loops reduce repetition.
Conditions add intelligence.
Retries improve reliability.
